[11:43.25]The United States Centers for Disease Control and Prevention reports that three million older people in America seek medical care for fall-related injuries each year. 
[11:53.88]But a doctor who specializes in treating old people says that we can prevent some falls.
[12:00.28]To prevent falls, doctors say people should have a reasonable perception about their balance as early as 50 years old. 
[12:07.71]If you feel unsteady, or lose balance, from time to time, ask your doctor to check all your body systems that can affect balance. 
[12:16.73]Along with muscle and bone problems, unsteadiness can also result from low blood pressure, inner-ear problems, nervous system issues and more. 
[12:26.45]You can test yourself easily at home to find out if you need any medical intervention. 
[12:32.28]Stand next to a wall or something that can provide support if needed. 
[12:36.69]Raise one leg. 
[12:37.97]Can you hold that position for 10 seconds£¿ 
[12:40.97]Now try the other leg.
[12:42.89]If you can stand on each leg alone for 10 seconds, you should be fine.
[12:47.88]Because muscle mass lessens in most people during their 30s, doctors who study and treat the diseases of old people say the best way to keep good balance is to stay physically active throughout life. 
[13:00.45]Activities including tai chi and yoga are good for older adults because they involve controlled movements while shifting body weight. 
[13:09.01]There also are simpler exercises people can work into their everyday lives. 
[13:14.65]The National Institute on Aging says people should spend at least 150 minutes per week doing physical activities to improve balance and overall health.
