[19:43.29]Recording Two
[19:44.88]As temperatures drop, a weighted blanket can be a popular choice to help you keep warm. 
[19:50.94]Some people say the extra pressure of the cover also helps to ease their worries and fall asleep faster. 
[19:57.68]However, research on the effectiveness of weighted blankets is limited. 
[20:02.52]So, what is a weighted blanket£¿
[20:05.20]Weighted blankets are filled with small glass balls, cotton or other fillings. 
[20:10.21]This makes them heavier than usual blankets. 
[20:13.34]Scientists may not know exactly how the blankets comfort, but they have a few ideas. 
[20:18.98]Sleep medicine experts say that the heavy feeling from a weighted blanket may calm the brain's fight-or-flight response. 
[20:26.26]The evenly distributed weight on you tells your body you are in a calm environment.
[20:31.74]However, there is little scientific research on whether weighted blankets help with sleep, anxiety, or other health issues. 
[20:40.10]The studies that exist involve small groups of people. 
[20:43.49]And most of the study subjects are people with conditions including sleep problems, long-term pain, and mental and developmental disorders.
[20:52.30]A study on 120 people with insomnia, an inability to get enough sleep, found that the weighted blankets helped them sleep better compared to a light blanket. 
[21:03.18]Another study considered 67 children with the developmental and brain disorder. 
[21:08.53]That research found that weighted blankets did not affect the children's sleep at all. 
[21:13.45]However, it also found that the study subjects liked the weighted blankets more than non-weighted blankets. 
[21:20.14]A third study on 94 adults with chronic pain found that a heavier weighted blanket did not affect sleep. 
[21:27.07]But it was better at reducing pain than a lighter blanket.
[21:30.84]Therefore, there is not much scientific evidence to support health improvement claims about weighted blankets. 
[21:37.67]However, in most cases they also cannot hurt, sleep experts say. 
[21:43.51]Dr. Daniel Barone is a sleep medicine expert at Weill Cornell Medicine in New York City. 
[21:50.04]He suggests the use of weighted blankets to his patients after they try other traditional treatments and medicines. 
[21:57.18]When choosing a weighted blanket, experts suggest one that is about 10 percent of your body weight. 
[22:03.15]However, people with breathing problems or disorders should check with a doctor before using a weighted blanket. 
[22:10.29]Also, weighted blankets are not suitable for children under three years old. 
[22:15.53]The weight can limit their movement and breathing.
[22:20.20]Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[22:25.85]19. How do weighted blankets help people relax according to sleep medicine experts£¿
[22:47.80]20. What did the study on 94 adults show about weighted blankets£¿
